# `linprog::success`

Whether a status counts as a usable solution: `Optimal` or `NearOptimal`.

## Signature

```cpp
bool linprog::success(linprog::Status status);
```

## What It Does

A lane ends `Optimal` when the primal and dual residuals and the gap are below `tolerance`, and `NearOptimal` when the iteration stalled at the limit of attainable accuracy but the residuals and gap are below the looser `accept_tolerance`; the best iterate is returned. Both carry a solution you can use, and `success` is true for both. It is false for `PrimalInfeasible`, `Unbounded` and `IterationLimit`, which carry a certificate or a failure rather than an answer. Use it as the one test before reading `x`.

## Parameters

`linprog::Status status`
: The status to test.

## Returns

`true` for `Optimal` and `NearOptimal`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

All five statuses.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace linprog = pluss::subsp::math::linprog;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  for (const linprog::Status status : {linprog::Status::Optimal, linprog::Status::NearOptimal, linprog::Status::PrimalInfeasible, linprog::Status::Unbounded, linprog::Status::IterationLimit}) {
    out.writeln(linprog::to_string(status), ": success =", linprog::success(status));
  }
}
```

### Example 2

Gating on a solve. A feasible problem passes; an infeasible one does not, and its Farkas multipliers are read instead.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace linprog = pluss::subsp::math::linprog;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  linprog::Problem problem;
  problem.c = pluss::series::make({1.0, 1.0});
  problem.a_ub = pluss::series::make({{1.0, 1.0}, {-1.0, -1.0}});
  problem.b_ub = pluss::series::make({1.0, -3.0});
  problem.lower = pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0});
  const linprog::Result result = linprog::solve(problem);
  const linprog::Status status = static_cast<linprog::Status>(static_cast<int>(result.status.item<double>()));
  if (linprog::success(status)) {
    out.writeln("solution x =", result.x.items<double>());
  } else {
    out.writeln("no solution:", linprog::to_string(status), ", farkas =", result.farkas.items<double>());
  }
}
```

### Example 3

`NearOptimal` is accepted. A lane that stalled close to optimal still counts, which is why `success` is the right test and a comparison with `Optimal` is too strict.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace linprog = pluss::subsp::math::linprog;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const linprog::Status stalled = linprog::Status::NearOptimal;
  out.writeln("== Optimal :", stalled == linprog::Status::Optimal);
  out.writeln("success   :", linprog::success(stalled));
}
```

